.TH "GIT-FOREST" "1" "2026-08-10" "git-forest" "Git Manual"
.SH NAME
git-forest \- manage named workspaces across multiple Git repositories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git forest
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ command ]
.br
.B git-forest
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ command ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git-forest
manages one named workspace across multiple configured canonical repositories
using linked Git worktrees.
Git exposes the installed executable as both
.B git-forest
and
.BR "git forest" .
.PP
With no command, Forest opens an interactive workspace launcher.
The
.B open
command starts the same launcher explicitly.
Without an interactive terminal, invoking Forest without a command prints its
command-line help.
.PP
Forest treats Git worktree metadata and the filesystem as authoritative.
It does not maintain a separate registry, reset or delete branches, or perform
implicit network operations.
Only
.BR setup ,
.BR fetch ,
and
.B update
contact remotes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help.
When using the Git-style command, Git handles
.B git forest \-\-help
itself by opening this manual page.
Use
.B git forest \-h
for concise command-line help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display the git-forest version.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Use an explicit configuration file instead of configuration discovery.
This option is global and may be used with any command.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit structured JSON.
This option is available on non-interactive commands.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B open
Search, create, and open workspaces interactively.
Press Space to select one or more existing workspaces, then Ctrl-D or Delete to
choose Archive, Force archive, Delete, or Force delete for the selection.
With no selection, the action applies to the highlighted workspace.
A single confirmation covers the full selection.
Archive preserves workspace-local files beneath
.BR .archive ;
Delete permanently removes them.
Non-force actions refuse dirty worktrees, force actions explicitly discard dirty
worktree changes, and every action preserves Git branches.
.TP
.B setup
Ensure configured canonical repositories are cloned.
Existing Git worktrees are reused without fetching or changing their remotes.
.TP
.B repos
List configured repositories, their origin URLs, and their locally known
default refs.
.TP
.BI fetch " [repository ...]"
Fetch
.B origin
for all configured repositories or for the named subset.
Use
.BI \-\-jobs " N"
to limit concurrent fetches.
.TP
.BI update " [repository ...]"
Fetch and fast-forward canonical default branches for all configured
repositories or for the named subset.
Updates are fast-forward only and never reset or force a branch.
Use
.BI \-\-jobs " N"
to limit concurrent fetches.
.TP
.BI create " workspace [checkout ...]"
Create a workspace, optionally with linked worktrees.
When no checkouts are provided, create an empty workspace directory so
worktrees can be added later with
.BR add .
A checkout is
.B repository
for its primary worktree or
.B repository@slot
for an additional worktree from the same repository.
Use
.BI \-\-base " checkout=ref"
to override a creation base and
.BI \-\-branch " checkout=branch"
to select an existing local branch or create one from its remote-tracking ref.
Use
.BI \-\-symbol " symbol"
to prefix the Herdr workspace name with an emoji or symbol and a space, and
.BI \-\-parent " workspace"
to record an active workspace as this workspace's parent.
Parents may nest to any depth; a parent that would form a cycle is rejected.
Both values are stored in the optional workspace-local
.B .forest-workspace.toml
file as
.B symbol
and
.BR parent ,
which may be edited by hand and is preserved across rename, archive, and
unarchive.
Symbols must be nonempty and contain no whitespace or control characters;
composed emoji are supported.
Repeating creation with either option updates that value; omitting it preserves
the saved value. Directory and branch names are unaffected.
A legacy
.B .forest-symbol
file is still read and is replaced the next time Forest writes metadata.
Creation preflights every requested checkout before mutation and is safe to
repeat after partial execution.
Archived names remain available: creation starts a fresh active workspace and
leaves saved files untouched, though existing branches may be reused.
Use
.B unarchive
to restore saved workspace files.
.TP
.BI add " workspace checkout ..."
Add primary or named linked worktrees to an existing workspace.
It accepts the same
.B \-\-base
and
.B \-\-branch
options as
.BR create .
.TP
.B list [\-\-archived]
List active workspaces by reconciling workspace directories with Git worktree
metadata.
Human output nests workspaces under their parents; JSON reports each
.BR parent .
With
.BR \-\-archived ,
list archives instead, with workspace name, human-readable date, archive ID,
and path.
Legacy archives show
.B legacy
instead of a date.
JSON output uses an
.B archives
array containing
.BR workspace ,
.BR archive_id ,
and
.B archive_path
fields rather than the active
.B workspaces
array.
.TP
.BI status " [workspace]"
Show workspace and worktree status, including dirty state, upstream, and
ahead/behind counts.
.TP
.BI path " workspace"
Print the absolute path of an existing workspace.
.TP
.BI attach " workspace"
Open an existing workspace in a running Herdr session as a single tab rooted in
the workspace directory.
If the workspace is already open, Forest focuses it.
Otherwise, if the Herdr workspace named by
.B HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID
has the workspace's parent open, Forest adds a tab there labeled with its
position, the workspace's symbol if any, and its name.
Otherwise, Forest creates a Herdr workspace whose only tab is
.BR 1-main .
A saved symbol prefixes the Herdr workspace name and is reconciled on each
attachment. Without a symbol, existing Herdr workspace names are left alone.
A missing parent produces a warning; cyclic parents are rejected.
Every descendant also opens as a tab in the same Herdr workspace, depth-first in
name order; descendants that are already open stay where they are.
Every workspace in the tree is checked before Herdr is contacted, and rerunning
after a failure opens only what is missing.
.TP
.BI rename " workspace new-workspace"
Rename an active workspace without changing its branches or working tree
contents.
Forest preflights the complete workspace, atomically moves its directory, and
uses
.B git worktree repair
to update worktree metadata in each canonical repository.
Dirty files and workspace-local entries are preserved.
An inconsistent source or active destination prevents the rename.
Archives do not reserve names and remain untouched.
If Git repair fails after the directory moves, repeating the same command
resumes the incomplete repair.
Before moving anything, rename updates
.B parent
in each active child, restoring it if the directory move fails.
Forest does not update Herdr runtime metadata.
.TP
.BI archive " workspace [\-\-force]"
Remove every clean registered worktree in a workspace, preserve its branches,
and move its workspace-local entries to a new generation beneath
.BR workspaces.root/.archive/.generations .
For example:
.BR .archive/.generations/nkdb-azure\-\-2026-09-26-09-46-11-0700 .
IDs use local time and its UTC offset in YYYY-MM-DD-HH-MM-SS\(+-HHMM form;
same-second collisions append -2, -3, and so on.
Older generations remain untouched.
Legacy
.B .archive/workspace
directories remain supported with ID
.BR legacy .
The separate
.B .generations
directory disambiguates timestamp-looking legacy workspace names.
There is no manifest or database.
Within a workspace, archives are ordered by their actual instant and numeric
collision suffix, with legacy archives first.
Archival rejects dirty or unregistered worktrees and workspaces with active
children, never overwrites files, and is safe to repeat after partial execution.
Use
.B \-\-force
only to explicitly discard modified, untracked, or ignored worktree files.
Repeating a successful archive reports it as already archived while no new
active workspace exists, selecting the newest generation or the legacy archive
if it is the only one.
Archives are excluded from the active workspace list and launcher; use
.B list \-\-archived
to see them.
The JSON archive report includes
.B archive_id
alongside the existing fields.
.TP
.BI unarchive " workspace [checkout ...] [\-\-archive ID] [\-\-as workspace]"
Restore saved workspace-local files, optionally creating only the requested
checkouts; previous checkouts are not recreated automatically.
It accepts the same
.B \-\-base
and
.B \-\-branch
options as
.BR create .
Use
.BI \-\-symbol " symbol"
and
.BI \-\-parent " workspace"
to override the saved symbol or parent when restoring; otherwise they are
preserved.
A single archive is selected automatically; multiple archives require
.BR "\-\-archive ID" .
Use ID
.B legacy
for an older archive, and
.B \-\-as
to restore under a different active workspace name.
Forest preflights all requested checkouts before moving files, refuses an
existing active destination, and never overwrites files.
Restoration consumes the selected archive and leaves other generations
untouched.
If checkout creation fails after the move, repeat the command.
When no matching archive remains and the destination is active,
Forest reconciles requested checkouts and reports
.BR already_active .
It never merges an archive into an active workspace.
Without a manifest, it cannot verify the workspace's origin or distinguish a
well-formed nonexistent ID from a consumed one.
Malformed IDs are rejected before reconciliation.
Invalid IDs and ambiguous selections return conflict reports.
The JSON report contains
.BR source_workspace ,
.BR workspace ,
.BR path ,
.BR archive_id ,
.BR archive_path ,
.BR repositories ,
.BR status ,
and
.BR message .
Status is
.BR unarchived ,
.BR already_active ,
.BR conflict ,
or
.BR failed .
Checkout reports use the same fields as
.BR create .
The archive path records the selected source, even after its move; it is null
on a retry after consumption.
The archive ID is null when none is selected or supplied.
.TP
.BI delete " workspace [\-\-force]"
Permanently delete a workspace while preserving its Git branches.
Forest first applies the same complete-workspace preflight and worktree removal
as
.BR archive ,
then stages workspace-local files at
.B .archive/.deleting/workspace
and permanently removes them.
Pending deletions are excluded from archive discovery and cannot be restored
with
.BR unarchive .
Dirty or unregistered worktrees and active child workspaces prevent deletion.
Use
.B \-\-force
only to explicitly discard modified, untracked, or ignored files in registered
worktrees; it does not delete branches or bypass unregistered-path checks.
Deleting an active workspace preserves older archives of that name.
If removal fails after staging, repeat the command to remove the pending files.
If a new active workspace has reused the name, retry refuses both paths until
you rename the active workspace.
With no active workspace, remaining registrations, or pending deletion, the
workspace is reported as already deleted.
Deletion never falls back to retained archives.
To delete an archive, including a legacy archive, restore it with
.B unarchive \-\-archive ID
(optionally using
.BR \-\-as )
and then delete the restored workspace.
The JSON
.B deleted_entries
field lists deletion staging paths of removed workspace-local entries,
excluding retained archives.
.TP
.B clean
Remove stale Git registrations for manually deleted Forest worktrees.
Forest uses
.B git worktree remove
for registrations beneath the active workspace root whose paths no longer
exist.
It preserves branches, ignores present worktrees, and does not affect stale
registrations outside the configured workspace root or beneath
.BR .archive .
The command is safe to repeat.
.TP
.BI remove " workspace [checkout ...]"
Remove all worktrees in a workspace or only the named checkouts.
Removal rejects dirty or unregistered paths, uses
.B git worktree remove
for present worktrees and stale registrations whose paths are already missing,
preserves branches, and requires
.B \-\-force
before discarding modified, untracked, or ignored files.
Workspace-local files, including
.BR .forest-workspace.toml ,
are reported and preserved; only an empty workspace directory with no active
child workspaces is removed.
Use
.B delete
to remove the workspace and its local files.
.TP
.BI completions " shell"
Generate dynamic shell completion setup for
.BR bash ,
.BR elvish ,
.BR fish ,
.BR powershell ,
or
.BR zsh .
This command does not require a Forest configuration.
.PP
Forest serializes
.BR create ,
.BR add ,
.BR rename ,
.BR archive ,
.BR unarchive ,
.BR delete ,
.BR clean ,
and
.B remove
mutations for a configuration so they cannot race one another.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
Evaluate the generated setup from the shell startup file.
For Bash or Zsh:
.PP
.EX
source <(git-forest completions bash)
.EE
.PP
Use
.B zsh
instead of
.B bash
for Zsh.
For Fish:
.PP
.EX
git-forest completions fish | source
.EE
.PP
Bash, Zsh, and Fish complete both
.B git-forest
and
.BR "git forest" .
Dynamic candidates include configured repositories, existing workspaces, and
workspace checkouts where appropriate.
Completion honors normal configuration discovery,
.BR FOREST_CONFIG ,
and
.BR \-\-config .
It never contacts remotes, mutates worktrees, or invokes Herdr.
Regenerate the setup after upgrading Forest.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Forest reads a
.B .forest.toml
file with a supported version, configured repositories, and workspace path and
branch templates.
The
.B .archive
directory directly beneath the configured workspace root is reserved for local
archived workspace contents.
Unless
.B \-\-config
or
.B FOREST_CONFIG
is set, Forest searches from the current directory toward the filesystem root.
.PP
Paths in the file are relative to the directory containing it.
The repository remote template may use
.BR {name} ,
and the workspace branch template may use
.B {workspace}
and
.BR {checkout} .
The latter is the named slot when present and otherwise the workspace name.
See the git-forest README for the complete format and policy.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FOREST_CONFIG
Path to the configuration file.
It has lower precedence than
.BR \-\-config .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colored human-readable output.
.TP
.B PATH
Used to find Git and, for
.BR attach ,
the Herdr executable.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including fully idempotent operations.
.TP
.B 1
An operational conflict or a Git, Herdr, or filesystem failure.
.TP
.B 2
A usage, input, or configuration error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR git-worktree (1)
